WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.439
[ Watch on KissFlix.co ]

2
00:04.709 --> 00:08.446
The Hakone Ekiden was founded in 1920.

3
00:08.513 --> 00:12.383
For the past 40 years, Dainichi TV has broadcast it.

4
00:12.450 --> 00:14.853
Chief producer Ryo Tokushige

5
00:14.919 --> 00:17.355
and broadcast director Natsuki Miyamoto

6
00:17.489 --> 00:20.792
prepare amid pressure from upper management.

7
00:20.859 --> 00:23.228
Hakone Ekiden belongs to the runners.

8
00:23.294 --> 00:26.898
Meanwhile, the Student United Team

9
00:26.965 --> 00:29.067
is made up of runners whose teams failed to qualify…

10
00:29.134 --> 00:31.870
My goal is a top-three finish at the Hakone Ekiden.

11
00:31.936 --> 00:35.173
The team is split over the policies of an inexperienced head coach

12
00:35.240 --> 00:36.674
Masato Kai.

13
00:36.741 --> 00:39.477
If you don't go, I won't either.

14
00:39.544 --> 00:41.746
Kai also had a fateful reunion

15
00:41.813 --> 00:44.449
with Tozai University head coach Hirakawa.

16
00:44.516 --> 00:48.553
"A great player doesn't always make a great coach."

17
00:48.620 --> 00:50.121
Then…

18
00:50.188 --> 00:52.590
"Abolish the Student United Team"?

19
00:53.358 --> 00:57.629
They refuse to stay down. How will this clash of convictions unfold?

20
01:04.736 --> 01:05.603
"I've been saying for years

21
01:05.670 --> 01:06.538
‎HAYATO AOBA, DAN KURASHINA ‎TENMA ISAYAMA, JO INOMATA

22
01:06.604 --> 01:10.141
that we need to rethink

23
01:10.208 --> 01:11.943
the Hakone Ekiden itself."

24
01:12.010 --> 01:15.246
"I question the point of letting

25
01:15.313 --> 01:16.481
a Student United Team

26
01:16.548 --> 01:18.116
with no record or meaning compete.

27
01:18.183 --> 01:20.818
How are the runners supposed to stay motivated?"

28
01:20.885 --> 01:22.554
Instead of adding a team like that,

29
01:22.620 --> 01:24.889
they should let another strong school run.

30
01:24.956 --> 01:25.823
HEAD COACH OF TOZAI UNIVERSITY SHOSUKE HIRAKAWA

31
01:25.890 --> 01:30.061
And Mr. Kai has no coaching experience.

32
01:30.128 --> 01:32.230
All you can do is laugh.

33
01:32.764 --> 01:36.834
He has no idea what it means

34
01:36.901 --> 01:40.872
to give your all at Hakone. It almost feels like he's mocking it.

35
01:41.606 --> 01:43.708
Hakone would be far better off

36
01:43.775 --> 01:46.477
if teams like that were abolished.

37
01:49.547 --> 01:50.949
What is this?

38
01:51.015 --> 01:53.051
That really hurts…

39
01:53.117 --> 01:57.622
It carries weight. Tozai University is a favorite to win.

40
02:04.762 --> 02:05.863
Guys,

41
02:07.265 --> 02:11.703
people might say things to us because of this.

42
02:11.769 --> 02:14.072
But what we need to do now

43
02:14.672 --> 02:17.175
is get properly ready for Hakone.

44
02:19.510 --> 02:21.145
Let's reset and move on.

45
02:21.212 --> 02:23.147
-Okay. -Okay.

46
02:34.826 --> 02:35.727
‎SITE INSPECTION OF THE RELAY POINTS

47
02:35.793 --> 02:39.397
Wow. Hakone really uses this many cameras.

48
02:39.464 --> 02:40.498
LIST OF CAMERA LOCATIONS

49
02:40.565 --> 02:45.169
The Hakone Ekiden broadcast links footage from more than 80 cameras

50
02:45.236 --> 02:48.239
through more than 40 receiving points.

51
02:48.906 --> 02:52.076
The broadcast signal is easily blocked

52
02:52.143 --> 02:53.978
by mountains and buildings.

53
02:54.045 --> 02:58.016
So a careful survey before the main race is a must every year.

54
02:58.783 --> 03:01.119
Is that Coach Hirakawa's article on the Student United Team?

55
03:01.185 --> 03:02.353
CHIEF PRODUCER-RYO TOKUSHIGE

56
03:02.420 --> 03:03.688
BROADCAST DIRECTOR-NATSUKI MIYAMOTO

57
03:03.755 --> 03:06.691
"Coach Kai spent years away from track

58
03:06.758 --> 03:10.828
working in business. He's basically an amateur."

59
03:10.895 --> 03:14.632
The reigning champ sure is harsh.

60
03:15.533 --> 03:19.237
Does Coach Hirakawa still see Mr. Kai as a rival?

61
03:19.304 --> 03:21.406
Yeah…

62
03:21.472 --> 03:23.841
Well, it's a bit of a legend.

63
03:23.908 --> 03:25.610
What is?

64
03:25.677 --> 03:29.614
In Hirakawa's last Hakone as a runner,

65
03:29.681 --> 03:33.184
freshman Kai easily passed him

66
03:33.251 --> 03:35.620
on the prestigious second leg.

67
03:35.687 --> 03:40.858
He also took the fastest time on the leg, and Meisei Gakuin took the overall title

68
03:40.925 --> 03:41.893
DIRECTOR-TOUGO MATSUZAWA

69
03:41.959 --> 03:43.161
Is this it?

70
03:43.227 --> 03:45.330
This one.

71
03:45.396 --> 03:48.266
Huh? Is the blurry guy Coach Hirakawa?

72
03:48.333 --> 03:51.302
He'd hold a grudge over this.

73
03:53.304 --> 03:54.872
DAINICHI TV

74
03:59.277 --> 04:00.311
Wait,

75
04:00.845 --> 04:04.349
the camera point on Komagatake is way up near the summit.

76
04:04.415 --> 04:05.717
We're not climbing it, are we?

77
04:05.783 --> 04:08.553
Relax. It's seven minutes by ropeway.

78
04:08.619 --> 04:11.823
Oh, there's a ropeway? I'm glad.

79
04:11.889 --> 04:14.525
What do you mean

80
04:14.592 --> 04:16.461
-the ropeway's not running? -What?

81
04:16.527 --> 04:17.729
It's my fault?

82
04:17.795 --> 04:21.632
Isn't checking this stuff production's job?

83
04:21.699 --> 04:24.001
Seriously. Nobody told us about this…

84
04:24.068 --> 04:25.236
Well…

85
04:25.870 --> 04:27.605
We're heading up.

86
04:27.672 --> 04:29.173
We'll get there eventually.

87
04:29.240 --> 04:30.975
-Matsuzawa! -Yes?

88
04:31.042 --> 04:32.276
Your salary has been reduced.

89
04:33.344 --> 04:35.046
That's harassment!

90
04:39.050 --> 04:42.186
How much farther to the top?

91
04:42.253 --> 04:44.989
About one hour.

92
04:45.056 --> 04:46.057
One hour?

93
04:50.361 --> 04:52.630
-Toyama. -Yes?

94
04:52.697 --> 04:55.767
Broadcast signals are a real pain.

95
04:56.601 --> 04:57.935
Back in the day,

96
05:00.071 --> 05:03.608
even something like this leaf blocked them.

97
05:04.442 --> 05:05.309
Oh…

98
05:05.877 --> 05:07.044
Take a look.

99
05:07.612 --> 05:11.482
People always said a full broadcast of the Hakone Ekiden was impossible.

100
05:12.350 --> 05:14.252
These mountains are why.

101
05:14.318 --> 05:18.489
-They all block the signal. -The crew back then spent days

102
05:18.556 --> 05:23.494
in the mountains looking for spots.

103
05:24.562 --> 05:26.397
I heard they climbed trackless slopes.

104
05:26.464 --> 05:29.500
It was truly life or death.

105
05:29.567 --> 05:31.936
That's beyond dedication.

106
05:32.570 --> 05:35.440
Master the tech, and you master Hakone!

107
05:36.274 --> 05:38.509
-Right, Mr. Kuwa? -Yeah.

108
05:39.177 --> 05:40.178
Okay, let's go!

109
05:40.244 --> 05:42.313
What, already?

110
05:44.816 --> 05:45.850
Wow.

111
05:46.517 --> 05:51.155
KOMAGATAKE SUMMIT

112
05:58.963 --> 06:01.098
-Was it around here? -Yeah.

113
06:03.234 --> 06:05.169
-Want to take a break? -Yeah.

114
06:05.236 --> 06:07.171
-We'll get some good shots here. -Yes.

115
06:07.905 --> 06:10.174
I hope we get good weather.

116
06:10.241 --> 06:12.944
-Pan wider left and right. -Okay.

117
06:15.513 --> 06:18.182
-There. Too far. -Okay.

118
06:18.249 --> 06:19.984
-Go back. -Okay.

119
06:20.785 --> 06:24.021
We could use this shot at the start of the return race.

120
06:24.622 --> 06:26.891
I'll use it behind the runner introduction captions.

121
06:27.391 --> 06:28.659
Just for that?

122
06:28.726 --> 06:30.561
Talk about a waste!

123
06:31.229 --> 06:33.464
Thank you for waiting. We're okay here.

124
06:33.531 --> 06:36.234
This spot's the same as last year, right?

125
06:36.300 --> 06:37.835
-Yeah. -Okay.

126
06:37.902 --> 06:39.704
Then shall we move on?

127
06:39.770 --> 06:40.838
-Okay. -Oh.

128
06:40.905 --> 06:42.073
Can I have a moment?

129
06:45.409 --> 06:47.211
PRIVATE PROPERTY - NO TRESPASSING

130
06:47.278 --> 06:50.915
There was an old apartment here until last year.

131
06:50.982 --> 06:52.783
They tore it down, and now it's an empty lot.

132
06:53.818 --> 06:57.488
Last time, there was a close race before the Hiratsuka sash relay.

133
06:57.555 --> 06:59.824
But we had no camera there and couldn't film it.

134
06:59.891 --> 07:02.827
I've been wanting to do something about that.

135
07:02.894 --> 07:06.030
-You mean put a new camera here? -Yeah.

136
07:06.097 --> 07:09.400
If we put a camera on a scaffold tower back there, we could see the whole course.

137
07:10.701 --> 07:12.203
That's tough.

138
07:13.704 --> 07:16.407
This is on the opposite side from the Hiratsuka Relay Point.

139
07:17.542 --> 07:19.377
What about the camera cable?

140
07:19.977 --> 07:23.447
We can't run it across a road with this much traffic.

141
07:27.385 --> 07:30.988
Toyama, could you look into permission to use this land for now?

142
07:31.055 --> 07:32.423
-Huh? -Miyamoto.

143
07:33.457 --> 07:37.061
I'm sorry. But I really want to try it once.

144
07:40.631 --> 07:43.634
Okay. We'll consider this spot.

145
07:44.302 --> 07:45.269
Let's move on.

146
07:46.704 --> 07:50.207
That's a brutal headwind. It's pretty strong.

147
07:50.274 --> 07:51.409
‎LEG COURSE INSPECTION ‎WALK THE COURSE LIKE THE FINAL RACE

148
07:51.475 --> 07:55.780
Meanwhile, the Student United Team runners

149
07:55.846 --> 07:57.715
scouted their candidate legs.

150
07:59.517 --> 08:00.985
OISO PINE TREE AVENUE

151
08:01.052 --> 08:03.554
This is it. The fourth leg's row of pine trees.

152
08:03.621 --> 08:04.455
‎TOKAIDO PINE TREE AVENUE

153
08:04.522 --> 08:05.756
Here we are.

154
08:06.557 --> 08:07.925
FOURTH AND SEVENTH LEGS-

155
08:07.992 --> 08:09.327
HIRATSUKA TO ODAWARA

156
08:09.393 --> 08:10.661
FOURTH LEG-20.9 KM

157
08:10.728 --> 08:12.530
"SECOND ACE LEG" A COURSE OF ROLLING HILLS

158
08:12.597 --> 08:14.865
Haru. Here.

159
08:14.932 --> 08:16.133
Send this to your family.

160
08:17.802 --> 08:19.403
-Thanks. -Hey.

161
08:19.470 --> 08:20.671
Look this way, you two.

162
08:21.238 --> 08:22.673
Here we go.

163
08:23.541 --> 08:25.343
-Yeah. -Okay.

164
08:25.409 --> 08:26.844
Say cheese.

165
08:28.846 --> 08:29.914
YOKOHAMA, GONTASAKA

166
08:29.981 --> 08:32.083
This hill is so long.

167
08:32.750 --> 08:34.986
And it's late in the race too…

168
08:35.586 --> 08:38.689
The second leg means picking off runners one after another.

169
08:39.256 --> 08:41.392
Okay. Keisuke Nogi,

170
08:41.459 --> 08:42.593
how many do you want to pass?

171
08:42.660 --> 08:44.028
NINTH LEG-23.1 KM “KEY RETURN LEG”

172
08:44.095 --> 08:46.564
-Ideally, five or six. -Oh!

173
08:47.264 --> 08:48.466
That's the spirit.

174
08:49.433 --> 08:50.601
What about you, Daichi?

175
08:51.702 --> 08:55.306
If I pass two, I'll be happy.

176
08:56.374 --> 08:58.075
Realistic.

177
08:58.142 --> 09:00.044
One look at this course and you would be.

178
09:01.078 --> 09:04.048
But that's what makes it fun.

179
09:05.149 --> 09:08.386
You charge straight up here.

180
09:08.452 --> 09:11.756
It would feel amazing.

181
09:14.759 --> 09:18.262
Wait, why do they say "picking off"?

182
09:22.733 --> 09:25.736
Hakone's famous hairpin curve!

183
09:25.803 --> 09:27.338
This gets me fired up.

184
09:27.405 --> 09:31.242
We've arrived at Ohiradai. Dan Kurashina…

185
09:31.308 --> 09:32.176
HAKONE, OHIRADAI

186
09:32.243 --> 09:34.078
-…and Jo Inomata. -Huh?

187
09:34.145 --> 09:36.313
They call us the Hill Brothers.

188
09:36.380 --> 09:37.848
What are we, a struggling comedy duo?

189
09:37.915 --> 09:39.316
Hello!

190
09:39.383 --> 09:42.453
Life's all uphill, and so is Hakone!

191
09:42.520 --> 09:43.821
I'm Dan Kurashina!

192
09:43.888 --> 09:46.791
-And my partner… -I'm not playing along.

193
09:47.391 --> 09:50.027
-Still, it's beautiful. -Yeah.

194
09:50.628 --> 09:52.463
Seeing it in person really hits you.

195
09:52.530 --> 09:54.732
It might rival the hills in Nagasaki.

196
09:54.799 --> 09:55.633
FIFTH LEG-20.8 KM "MOUNTAIN CLIMB" SIXTH LEG-20.8 KM "MOUNTAIN DESCENT"

197
09:55.700 --> 09:57.334
Jo, you lived at the top of a hill, right?

198
09:57.902 --> 10:00.371
Yeah. Up and down every day.

199
10:00.938 --> 10:04.175
You really have lived with hills all your life.

200
10:04.241 --> 10:08.179
-Where was it again? -I came here a lot as a kid too.

201
10:08.245 --> 10:09.413
Really?

202
10:09.480 --> 10:10.581
TOKYO, OTEMACHI

203
10:10.648 --> 10:12.450
Found it!

204
10:12.516 --> 10:14.018
This is the finish line.

205
10:14.085 --> 10:15.019
-So this is it. -Here.

206
10:15.086 --> 10:16.087
Yeah.

207
10:16.854 --> 10:18.856
Hey, everyone!

208
10:18.923 --> 10:20.157
A FAST LEG

209
10:20.224 --> 10:21.158
Hey!

210
10:22.259 --> 10:24.462
Tenma, you guys are scouting today too?

211
10:24.528 --> 10:27.331
We got carried away and did the whole round trip.

212
10:27.398 --> 10:29.233
So? How was the first leg?

213
10:29.800 --> 10:31.936
Amazing.

214
10:32.002 --> 10:34.572
Makes me want to show what the Student United Team can do.

215
10:34.638 --> 10:37.007
-Oh? -Come on, everyone.

216
10:37.074 --> 10:40.044
Let's prove Coach Hirakawa wrong.

217
10:45.149 --> 10:46.450
Natsuki.

218
10:47.251 --> 10:49.353
-No luck with the Hiratsuka lot. -No?

219
10:50.087 --> 10:53.524
The elderly owner flatly refused to let us put a camera there.

220
10:53.591 --> 10:55.326
He shut me out completely.

221
10:55.392 --> 10:58.162
-Why? -I don't know.

222
10:58.229 --> 10:59.163
He just kept saying no.

223
11:00.264 --> 11:01.398
I see.

224
11:02.099 --> 11:03.601
Could you keep at it a little longer?

225
11:03.667 --> 11:06.437
Hey, give her a break.

226
11:06.504 --> 11:08.405
Toyama has other work too.

227
11:11.609 --> 11:12.443
What about Matsuzawa?

228
11:13.177 --> 11:17.448
I'm having trouble getting through to the transport ministry. Sorry…

229
11:17.515 --> 11:20.217
Everyone's busy.

230
11:20.284 --> 11:22.219
-Hashiguchi, you too, right? -Yes.

231
11:22.286 --> 11:25.890
Mr. Karashima asked for all the materials from the past 15 years…

232
11:25.956 --> 11:29.593
Hey. Can't the announcers handle Mr. Karashima's tasks?

233
11:29.660 --> 11:31.695
We're swamped with interviews, too.

234
11:34.331 --> 11:37.401
Toyama, could you give me the landowner's name and address?

235
11:39.370 --> 11:41.806
GOTO

236
11:41.872 --> 11:44.575
-Yes? -I'm sorry to drop by.

237
11:44.642 --> 11:47.211
I'm Miyamoto from Dainichi TV.

238
11:47.278 --> 11:49.380
I'm here about the Hakone Ekiden we discussed.

239
11:51.582 --> 11:52.616
LAND OWNER - JIRO GOTO

240
11:52.683 --> 11:53.551
No.

241
11:54.919 --> 11:58.222
I told that young one already. No cameras there.

242
11:58.989 --> 12:01.859
-Um, Mr. Goto… -No means no!

243
12:01.926 --> 12:03.160
Now go home!

244
12:07.765 --> 12:08.666
Good work.

245
12:08.732 --> 12:10.367
-Good work. -Oh!

246
12:10.434 --> 12:11.302
How did it go

247
12:11.368 --> 12:13.470
-with Mr. Goto? -No luck. He slammed the door on me.

248
12:13.537 --> 12:17.007
Same here! "No!" Bang!

249
12:17.074 --> 12:19.510
So stubborn old geezers still exist these days.

250
12:20.077 --> 12:22.213
Okay, that's ageist.

251
12:22.279 --> 12:25.249
Sorry. Stubborn old man. I mean Mr. Stubborn old men.

252
12:25.316 --> 12:26.684
That's the same thing.

253
12:27.585 --> 12:28.752
There you are.

254
12:29.420 --> 12:31.155
-Miyamoto, -Yes?

255
12:31.222 --> 12:32.489
About the Hiratsuka cable…

256
12:33.057 --> 12:36.393
-Yes. -We looked into it,

257
12:36.460 --> 12:37.595
but it's not going to work.

258
12:38.329 --> 12:41.599
Even with rubber mats, that traffic makes it dangerous.

259
12:41.665 --> 12:45.236
If a big truck runs over it and cuts it, we're finished.

260
12:47.204 --> 12:50.341
-What if we use poles and run it overhead? -On a road that wide?

261
12:51.041 --> 12:53.978
What if the cable sags and catches on a truck?

262
12:55.779 --> 12:59.717
-Then-- -Tech agrees that the camera positions

263
12:59.783 --> 13:00.985
we have now are best.

264
13:04.188 --> 13:06.423
-For example-- -Listen, Miyamoto!

265
13:07.791 --> 13:10.027
Tech can't do everything!

266
13:11.095 --> 13:12.496
It can't be done. Period.

267
13:13.664 --> 13:15.666
Knowing when to give up is part of your job, too.

268
13:20.938 --> 13:23.040
-Hey, Shige. -Yes?

269
13:23.874 --> 13:28.012
She's still hung up on that Hiratsuka camera point.

270
13:28.078 --> 13:29.780
Miyamoto?

271
13:29.847 --> 13:32.182
I've never seen anyone so stubborn.

272
13:33.117 --> 13:37.154
I have my hands full with her stubbornness, too.

273
13:38.088 --> 13:41.792
You were a lot like her back in the day.

274
13:43.394 --> 13:48.032
Well, you always found a way when I kept pushing.

275
13:49.566 --> 13:53.404
She's a handful, but please keep working with her.

276
13:54.071 --> 13:56.006
-Hey, Shige. -Yes?

277
13:56.774 --> 14:00.110
Tech can't do everything.

278
14:07.618 --> 14:10.688
-Okay. Just 2.5 minutes more. -Thank you.

279
14:10.754 --> 14:12.089
Mr. Tokushige.

280
14:12.156 --> 14:13.290
-Yeah? -Thanks for the food!

281
14:13.357 --> 14:15.893
-Thanks for the food! -You owe me for life!

282
14:16.760 --> 14:18.062
Not for one cup of instant ramen!

283
14:18.128 --> 14:21.432
MAP OF CAMERA LOCATION

284
14:27.037 --> 14:28.038
Miyamoto.

285
14:28.739 --> 14:31.508
I heard you got turned down on the Hiratsuka camera point.

286
14:32.242 --> 14:35.579
Yes. Mr. Kuwana told me to give up too.

287
14:37.614 --> 14:41.585
Stubborn old geezers gave me

288
14:42.186 --> 14:43.420
plenty of trouble too.

289
14:43.487 --> 14:45.055
What do you mean?

290
14:45.656 --> 14:49.126
Back when I started on Hakone.

291
14:49.193 --> 14:53.330
My first job was covering Meisei Gakuin.

292
14:53.897 --> 14:57.735
It was rough. Coach Moroya

293
14:58.402 --> 15:00.838
absolutely hated the media.

294
15:00.904 --> 15:04.141
21 YEARS AGO

295
15:05.342 --> 15:08.078
Hey! You!

296
15:08.946 --> 15:11.949
-Yes? -How many times do I have to tell you?

297
15:12.016 --> 15:13.650
You're in the way!

298
15:13.717 --> 15:15.853
Sorry! Just this one shot!

299
15:15.919 --> 15:18.355
-Please! -No means no!

300
15:18.422 --> 15:19.390
Just this…

301
15:19.456 --> 15:22.092
-He yelled at me every time I went. -Sorry.

302
15:22.159 --> 15:25.429
He was saying I hadn't done enough research.

303
15:25.496 --> 15:28.532
Or I didn't talk properly.

304
15:28.599 --> 15:30.434
So do you go to mixers and stuff?

305
15:30.968 --> 15:32.403
Sometimes.

306
15:32.469 --> 15:35.039
Wow!

307
15:35.105 --> 15:39.910
Then, if you win the Izumo Ekiden, I'll set one up.

308
15:39.977 --> 15:43.280
-For real?! -Shut up!

309
15:43.347 --> 15:44.615
Not a word to the coach.

310
15:45.816 --> 15:47.418
Tokushige!

311
15:51.055 --> 15:53.123
Yes. I'm leaving! Going now!

312
15:53.857 --> 15:57.327
-You really are relentless. -Yes.

313
15:57.961 --> 16:00.397
My boss calls me a grease stain.

314
16:00.464 --> 16:01.365
Right.

315
16:01.432 --> 16:02.933
Somebody get the detergent!

316
16:03.000 --> 16:04.068
MEISEI GAKUIN UNIVERSITY

317
16:04.134 --> 16:05.969
Please! Not detergent! Sorry!

318
16:06.036 --> 16:08.305
But I kept showing up,

319
16:08.372 --> 16:12.342
and he finally let me cover them. Eventually, Coach Moroya even

320
16:12.409 --> 16:15.145
started inviting me out to eat.

321
16:15.212 --> 16:17.881
-Listen, Tokushige. -Yes?

322
16:21.552 --> 16:25.122
The Hakone Ekiden is scary.

323
16:25.189 --> 16:26.190
Huh?

324
16:26.990 --> 16:30.360
Once it gets hold of you, you can never escape.

325
16:34.431 --> 16:39.603
I'm doomed to never escape Hakone until the day I die.

326
16:42.372 --> 16:46.143
If you're going to turn back, now's your chance.

327
16:50.247 --> 16:51.682
I'm sorry.

328
16:52.783 --> 16:54.651
I think

329
16:56.253 --> 16:57.855
it's already too late for me.

330
17:00.524 --> 17:02.626
I see.

331
17:02.693 --> 17:04.595
-Then drink. -Thank you.

332
17:06.864 --> 17:11.268
Doomed to never escape Hakone until the day I die, huh…

333
17:12.503 --> 17:13.337
Huh?

334
17:14.304 --> 17:18.208
No. Anyway, stubborn old geezers can be surprisingly moved

335
17:18.275 --> 17:21.545
by someone even more stubborn

336
17:21.612 --> 17:23.413
who refuses to quit.

337
17:35.459 --> 17:40.497
SECOND-YEAR STUDENT AT KANTO UNIVERSITY OF CULTURE-SEIYA NAITO

338
17:43.400 --> 17:45.002
You're always the first one out.

339
17:45.569 --> 17:48.138
HEAD COACH OF MEISEI GAKUIN UNIVERSITY MASATO KAI

340
17:48.205 --> 17:49.439
Your times have stalled?

341
17:53.510 --> 17:57.581
You're running so much that your movements have gotten smaller.

342
17:58.682 --> 18:00.817
Try running once without thinking about distance.

343
18:04.054 --> 18:05.522
This isn't just about Hakone.

344
18:06.924 --> 18:10.894
What I just said applies to your running itself.

345
18:13.597 --> 18:16.033
Whether it's a relay or a full marathon,

346
18:17.167 --> 18:18.702
you're the one running, right?

347
18:22.306 --> 18:23.607
Good morning.

348
18:24.908 --> 18:26.610
-Morning. -Excuse me.

349
18:29.680 --> 18:30.981
Did something happen with Seiya?

350
18:34.151 --> 18:36.687
No one rebels without a reason.

351
18:38.355 --> 18:40.557
Coach, got a minute?

352
18:40.624 --> 18:41.925
SEIWA INTERNATIONAL UNIV. COACH

353
18:41.992 --> 18:43.227
I need to talk to you.

354
18:44.261 --> 18:45.596
Yes.

355
18:47.731 --> 18:48.899
SHOSUKE HIRAKAWA

356
18:48.966 --> 18:52.169
Kai, I warned you over and over.

357
18:52.236 --> 18:53.403
SEIJIRO ONUMA

358
18:53.470 --> 18:58.175
Stop stirring up the public with an unrealistic goal.

359
19:00.477 --> 19:02.879
It's one thing if people point at us.

360
19:02.946 --> 19:09.453
But it's the runners who'll be laughed at and looked down on!

361
19:09.519 --> 19:12.923
It takes time to win the public over.

362
19:13.557 --> 19:16.059
I believe the runners understand that

363
19:16.126 --> 19:19.496
and train by their own choice.

364
19:21.031 --> 19:22.032
Right.

365
19:22.099 --> 19:24.201
Like you said once, Kitano,

366
19:24.268 --> 19:25.802
the runners are smart.

367
19:26.603 --> 19:30.807
We should just watch over them and trust them to take charge.

368
19:30.874 --> 19:34.778
I try not to believe in idealism with no basis.

369
19:34.845 --> 19:37.347
Oh, that's a shame.

370
19:40.183 --> 19:41.285
Listen, Kai.

371
19:41.918 --> 19:47.324
Stop wasting runners who have their whole futures ahead.

372
19:57.701 --> 20:00.704
Everyone has their own ideas and opinions.

373
20:01.872 --> 20:03.807
We have to accept that.

374
20:06.009 --> 20:09.012
But it's really hard.

375
20:10.681 --> 20:14.551
It's much easier to reject others than to accept them.

376
20:15.919 --> 20:17.988
But the answer comes out eventually.

377
20:19.556 --> 20:22.259
At least in the world of track and field.

378
20:24.695 --> 20:26.530
You're so persistent!

379
20:27.097 --> 20:28.298
I don't want a camera there.

380
20:28.365 --> 20:32.803
I wrote up where the camera would go and how we'd set it up.

381
20:32.869 --> 20:34.471
I promise it won't cause you any trouble.

382
20:37.641 --> 20:39.710
MAP OF CAMERA LOCATION

383
20:41.078 --> 20:44.614
Listen. Let me make this clear.

384
20:44.681 --> 20:49.486
I hate the Hakone Ekiden and the people who broadcast it!

385
20:50.887 --> 20:53.023
Could you tell me why?

386
20:53.090 --> 20:55.125
Think hard about it yourself!

387
21:10.140 --> 21:14.811
Mr. Goto, are you related to Hideki Goto from Showa International University?

388
21:15.379 --> 21:16.847
Hideki is my grandson.

389
21:18.081 --> 21:20.250
Now go home. Don't ever come back!

390
21:32.996 --> 21:36.500
Miyamoto. How did it go?

391
21:38.635 --> 21:41.138
It was my fault.

392
21:42.105 --> 21:43.106
Huh?

393
21:45.275 --> 21:47.577
Something's wrong with Hideki Goto

394
21:47.644 --> 21:49.846
of Showa International University. Oh.

395
21:49.913 --> 21:52.115
Is his leg okay? Did he hurt something?

396
21:52.182 --> 21:53.683
-Car Three, stay on him. -He has become

397
21:53.750 --> 21:54.851
unsteady on his feet.

398
21:54.918 --> 21:57.621
He can't get his legs moving the way he wants.

399
21:57.687 --> 22:00.757
-Hideki Goto. It's a little worrying. -Miyamoto.

400
22:00.824 --> 22:03.026
-It's obvious something is wrong. -What do you want to do?

401
22:03.093 --> 22:06.129
-As you can see, he's almost stopped. -We'll stay on him.

402
22:06.196 --> 22:09.166
His mouth is open, but he's desperately

403
22:09.232 --> 22:11.768
-swinging his arms. -I see.

404
22:11.835 --> 22:14.304
Hideki Goto's grandfather…

405
22:16.606 --> 22:17.874
I don't know anymore

406
22:18.708 --> 22:22.012
if I was right

407
22:22.946 --> 22:24.181
to keep filming him then.

408
22:26.283 --> 22:27.851
Right.

409
22:29.686 --> 22:31.655
Choosing a single shot

410
22:32.255 --> 22:34.324
is always hard.

411
22:35.358 --> 22:40.030
-Even for you, Mr. Tokushige? -Of course.

412
22:40.630 --> 22:42.933
But

413
22:43.567 --> 22:46.603
we still have to choose. Right?

414
22:50.340 --> 22:53.777
Um, I think I'll go see him once more.

415
22:57.681 --> 22:59.983
I wonder what Kota and the others want to talk about.

416
23:00.584 --> 23:02.652
Captain, do you know what it's about?

417
23:02.719 --> 23:04.087
No…

418
23:04.154 --> 23:05.322
‎Here they come.

419
23:12.796 --> 23:14.965
MEGURO UNIVERSITY OF EDUCATION SHUTO TOMIOKA

420
23:15.031 --> 23:17.300
SEIWA INTERNATIONAL UNIVERSITY KOTA MATSUKI

421
23:17.367 --> 23:18.235
This…

422
23:20.270 --> 23:22.706
You've all read this by now, right?

423
23:25.275 --> 23:28.778
Now I finally get it.

424
23:28.845 --> 23:32.115
This is why Coach Kai set

425
23:32.182 --> 23:35.018
that stupid top-three goal!

426
23:35.085 --> 23:36.520
In other words,

427
23:37.220 --> 23:40.690
it's a publicity stunt, right?

428
23:41.258 --> 23:44.728
He has no record as a coach, so he's being flashy for attention.

429
23:44.794 --> 23:48.398
And we're getting dragged into it!

430
23:50.066 --> 23:52.035
This is seriously messed up.

431
23:52.102 --> 23:53.370
In the first place,

432
23:54.137 --> 23:57.140
a position that doesn't go on record doesn't mean anything.

433
23:58.742 --> 24:01.211
Coach Hirakawa

434
24:01.778 --> 24:03.313
or Coach Kai.

435
24:04.948 --> 24:08.985
You all know who's right, don't you?

436
24:10.954 --> 24:12.289
So what do you want to do, Kota?

437
24:12.355 --> 24:14.858
I think we should all tell Coach Kai

438
24:14.925 --> 24:17.661
to drop the top-three goal!

439
24:19.095 --> 24:20.697
At Hakone,

440
24:20.764 --> 24:25.368
we should just run like a Student United Team!

441
24:30.907 --> 24:33.443
What does running like a Student United Team even mean?

442
24:36.646 --> 24:40.350
Isn't a team something you build, not something that just exists?

443
24:44.521 --> 24:47.724
When I first heard top three, I was worried too.

444
24:47.791 --> 24:49.492
I couldn't believe it either.

445
24:51.194 --> 24:55.599
But after getting together and training under Coach Kai,

446
24:56.600 --> 24:58.134
I feel differently now.

447
25:01.104 --> 25:04.874
I want to run Hakone with this team.

448
25:06.309 --> 25:08.411
And I want to aim for top three.

449
25:12.849 --> 25:13.783
Are you serious?

450
25:14.951 --> 25:16.052
I'm serious.

451
25:18.054 --> 25:18.955
Are you stupid?

452
25:19.022 --> 25:22.425
Can we not talk like that?

453
25:29.399 --> 25:30.767
For years…

454
25:33.737 --> 25:35.605
my school has worked

455
25:36.172 --> 25:38.308
its hardest to make Hakone.

456
25:41.144 --> 25:42.178
But…

457
25:43.446 --> 25:46.049
at the qualifying race, we got crushed and finished 18th.

458
25:47.484 --> 25:49.819
That's my reality.

459
25:51.254 --> 25:52.122
And

460
25:52.756 --> 25:54.991
now a team that's been together for just two months

461
25:55.625 --> 25:59.396
is aiming for top three with an amateur coach? Give me a break!

462
26:04.167 --> 26:05.335
Um…

463
26:06.569 --> 26:09.472
I agree with Haru.

464
26:11.207 --> 26:16.746
No, I just really like this team.

465
26:17.814 --> 26:22.052
So I want to run Hakone with this Student United Team.

466
26:22.752 --> 26:25.922
And I want all of us to reach Coach Kai's goal.

467
26:26.723 --> 26:31.795
Don't take what that coach says seriously.

468
26:32.362 --> 26:35.598
I take Coach Kai's words seriously too.

469
26:40.336 --> 26:41.638
Sure,

470
26:42.539 --> 26:44.774
Coach Kai can be unconventional.

471
26:46.076 --> 26:50.380
But it feels like he really watches us and thinks about us.

472
26:51.114 --> 26:53.216
Haruka! Stay! Don't speed up now!

473
26:53.950 --> 26:56.486
What he says

474
26:56.553 --> 26:57.987
always hits me.

475
27:00.490 --> 27:02.492
Whether it's a relay or a full marathon,

476
27:03.526 --> 27:04.894
you're the one doing the running.

477
27:08.665 --> 27:09.666
I ran into Coach Kai

478
27:10.900 --> 27:13.036
in the hallway earlier.

479
27:13.103 --> 27:16.406
He asked me what was going on.

480
27:18.308 --> 27:21.911
He said to talk it out ourselves until we're satisfied.

481
27:22.979 --> 27:24.247
Can I say something too?

482
27:29.085 --> 27:33.156
I came to college in Tokyo because I wanted to run Hakone.

483
27:34.791 --> 27:39.529
But my school failed to qualify two years in a row.

484
27:40.864 --> 27:45.568
So I really get how you feel, Shuto.

485
27:47.303 --> 27:48.638
But right now,

486
27:49.539 --> 27:53.042
I want to go to Hakone with this team.

487
27:53.109 --> 27:55.979
I want to show our supporters

488
27:56.045 --> 27:58.348
a Student United Team like never before.

489
27:58.948 --> 28:02.285
I want our running to surprise everyone.

490
28:05.522 --> 28:06.656
I agree.

491
28:08.324 --> 28:14.264
Honestly, unofficial times and no position

492
28:14.898 --> 28:18.535
don't matter to people watching.

493
28:20.603 --> 28:25.074
Let's all compete for real.

494
28:31.815 --> 28:34.684
Seiya, do you agree with Kota and Shuto?

495
28:45.862 --> 28:46.996
Does the Hakone Ekiden

496
28:49.365 --> 28:55.171
even mean that much in the first place?

497
28:56.439 --> 28:57.307
What do you mean?

498
29:00.577 --> 29:04.914
Hakone this, Hakone that. Isn't everyone making too much of it?

499
29:05.982 --> 29:09.118
For marathon runners, it's supposed to be just one stop.

500
29:09.185 --> 29:12.021
But everyone burns out at Hakone.

501
29:13.289 --> 29:15.358
Seiya, you want to run marathons?

502
29:18.294 --> 29:21.631
Eventually, I want to go that way.

503
29:23.132 --> 29:26.035
But I know where I stand.

504
29:26.102 --> 29:27.403
Honestly,

505
29:28.471 --> 29:30.974
my times aren't good enough to talk about dreams.

506
29:38.114 --> 29:39.616
Nobody's going to

507
29:41.117 --> 29:45.021
shoot down your dream.

508
29:48.324 --> 29:51.961
And not everyone running Hakone

509
29:52.862 --> 29:55.932
has to make Hakone their only goal.

510
29:57.967 --> 30:00.036
It's fine to think of it

511
30:00.770 --> 30:02.171
as just one stop, right?

512
30:05.642 --> 30:06.609
Yeah, that's right.

513
30:09.913 --> 30:15.618
Being on the same team doesn't mean we all have to think the same way.

514
30:17.887 --> 30:21.991
But if we're running, I think we should all give Hakone

515
30:22.692 --> 30:24.827
everything we have.

516
30:27.897 --> 30:33.536
We're here because we love running.

517
30:38.141 --> 30:39.943
I'm with the captain!

518
30:40.643 --> 30:42.211
Me too!

519
30:42.979 --> 30:45.148
-Me too! -Me too!

520
30:54.257 --> 30:56.159
Kota, Shuto, Seiya,

521
30:57.060 --> 30:59.395
could you think it over again?

522
31:01.064 --> 31:05.401
About what we should do as a team from here on.

523
31:13.042 --> 31:15.011
Today's practice is 5,000 meters.

524
31:15.578 --> 31:18.147
It was going to be a pace trial,

525
31:18.214 --> 31:19.782
but I'm changing it to a time trial.

526
31:20.984 --> 31:23.820
Show me how serious you are now.

527
31:37.333 --> 31:40.203
Is this team even worth getting serious for?

528
31:42.505 --> 31:43.706
It is.

529
31:47.543 --> 31:49.379
On your marks.

530
31:53.683 --> 31:56.019
Ready, go!

531
32:13.236 --> 32:14.771
29, 30,

532
32:14.871 --> 32:17.673
31, 32, 33

533
32:18.408 --> 32:21.477
34, 35, 36,

534
32:21.544 --> 32:23.279
37, 38…

535
32:27.483 --> 32:28.618
Coach!

536
33:04.053 --> 33:05.655
Okay!

537
33:14.497 --> 33:15.565
Fourteen minutes.

538
33:15.631 --> 33:18.601
Three seconds, four seconds, five seconds

539
33:18.668 --> 33:22.004
six, seven, eight, nine,

540
33:22.672 --> 33:24.073
10, 11,

541
33:24.841 --> 33:27.643
12, 13, 14,

542
33:27.710 --> 33:29.545
15, 16…

543
33:38.621 --> 33:39.589
Kota!

544
33:45.862 --> 33:46.963
Nice run.

545
33:59.575 --> 34:02.145
-Today was amazing. -Yeah.

546
34:03.846 --> 34:07.383
It feels like everyone's morale suddenly shot up.

547
34:13.089 --> 34:14.290
Good work.

548
34:14.991 --> 34:16.759
That was a great run.

549
34:28.871 --> 34:29.972
Um, Coach.

550
34:31.641 --> 34:32.842
What is it?

551
34:37.980 --> 34:39.182
About your advice yesterday.

552
34:40.783 --> 34:42.318
Thank you.

553
34:50.660 --> 34:51.794
Hey.

554
34:57.600 --> 35:00.002
Don't send mixed signals.

555
35:01.537 --> 35:03.005
Come to my office later.

556
35:07.243 --> 35:08.344
Okay.

557
35:11.714 --> 35:13.049
That's enough!

558
35:13.115 --> 35:15.618
How many times do I have to say no about the camera?

559
35:15.685 --> 35:17.253
No, that's not it!

560
35:17.820 --> 35:21.591
I'm not here about that today. I came to apologize.

561
35:22.158 --> 35:23.059
Apologize?

562
35:27.997 --> 35:32.902
I saw Goto run at the qualifying race.

563
35:35.104 --> 35:38.040
It was a truly wonderful run.

564
35:42.144 --> 35:44.080
When he slowed down,

565
35:45.848 --> 35:49.518
I'm the one who chose to keep filming him.

566
35:51.153 --> 35:55.224
Do you have any idea what he was going through?

567
35:57.360 --> 36:01.998
What was the point of showing him over and over, hurt and in pain?

568
36:02.064 --> 36:07.837
Did you think for one second about how he felt as he ran?

569
36:12.708 --> 36:15.745
You have every right to say that.

570
36:17.213 --> 36:18.414
Mr. Goto.

571
36:18.481 --> 36:20.716
To me, strength

572
36:21.717 --> 36:23.219
isn't just winning.

573
36:23.953 --> 36:26.922
It's taking on a challenge without fearing defeat.

574
36:28.224 --> 36:31.327
Win or lose, isn't that when people

575
36:32.028 --> 36:34.630
shine brightest, when they give their all?

576
36:37.533 --> 36:41.604
At the qualifying race, I didn't keep filming him

577
36:42.805 --> 36:46.075
to mock him or out of curiosity.

578
36:47.476 --> 36:49.312
We don't just praise the winners.

579
36:49.912 --> 36:53.749
We also show that the losers gave everything they had.

580
36:53.816 --> 36:57.086
I believe that's what

581
36:57.153 --> 36:58.888
true sports broadcasting is.

582
37:01.290 --> 37:02.491
But…

583
37:04.427 --> 37:09.832
whatever my intentions, I hurt someone.

584
37:10.633 --> 37:12.401
That's something that can never be excused.

585
37:14.003 --> 37:17.373
As the director, all the responsibility is mine.

586
37:19.075 --> 37:23.479
I'm truly sorry.

587
37:32.121 --> 37:35.691
Thank you for your time.

588
37:37.927 --> 37:40.496
Grandpa, I'm coming in.

589
37:41.330 --> 37:42.164
Hideki?

590
37:47.136 --> 37:48.104
Long time no see.

591
37:48.170 --> 37:49.572
HIDEKI GOTO

592
37:49.638 --> 37:50.606
Hello.

593
37:52.608 --> 37:53.743
What are you doing here?

594
37:56.178 --> 37:57.580
Please, come in.

595
38:00.850 --> 38:02.118
Excuse me.

596
38:02.852 --> 38:04.086
Mr. Tokushige?

597
38:08.023 --> 38:09.925
-My grandpa did? -Yes.

598
38:10.459 --> 38:14.263
Our director is visiting him in person to apologize.

599
38:14.797 --> 38:16.999
But I thought I should apologize

600
38:17.066 --> 38:19.435
to you first.

601
38:21.337 --> 38:22.405
As chief producer,

602
38:22.471 --> 38:25.841
I take full responsibility for the qualifying race.

603
38:26.575 --> 38:28.511
I'm truly…

604
38:29.211 --> 38:30.279
Mr. Tokushige.

605
38:31.781 --> 38:32.615
Yes?

606
38:33.482 --> 38:34.750
Let's go.

607
38:38.421 --> 38:39.522
It's true.

608
38:40.256 --> 38:42.057
After the qualifying race,

609
38:43.159 --> 38:45.461
I couldn't bring myself to watch it.

610
38:47.830 --> 38:49.165
But you know, Grandpa,

611
38:50.299 --> 38:53.969
a lot of people sent me messages after.

612
38:55.704 --> 38:57.273
They said they were moved.

613
39:02.678 --> 39:06.649
That broadcast is a record of me losing. No doubt.

614
39:08.284 --> 39:09.452
But more than that,

615
39:10.252 --> 39:11.420
it's a record of me

616
39:12.188 --> 39:14.623
not giving up the race.

617
39:24.200 --> 39:25.234
Grandpa.

618
39:25.301 --> 39:27.102
Thanks for worrying about me.

619
39:27.903 --> 39:30.906
But I'm okay now.

620
39:33.709 --> 39:36.078
This time, I'm going to run Hakone for sure.

621
39:39.348 --> 39:42.918
I'll pay Hakone back at Hakone.

622
39:57.533 --> 39:58.734
Use it however you like.

623
40:00.269 --> 40:02.338
You want to put a camera there, right?

624
40:04.273 --> 40:05.741
Use it however you like.

625
40:06.876 --> 40:08.010
But in return,

626
40:08.077 --> 40:10.880
film those runners properly.

627
40:12.581 --> 40:13.749
Yes!

628
40:17.453 --> 40:18.854
Thank you very much.

629
40:21.957 --> 40:25.060
Thank you, Mr. Tokushige.

630
40:28.097 --> 40:31.033
We'd better deliver the best shots, right?

631
40:31.767 --> 40:32.902
Yes.

632
40:36.539 --> 40:38.774
-There's still a problem. -Huh?

633
40:39.408 --> 40:41.143
The cable.

634
40:42.411 --> 40:46.148
Mr. Kuwa said there's no way to run it through there.

635
40:46.715 --> 40:50.519
Um, I was thinking of going to see him again now…

636
40:57.459 --> 40:59.728
Hello. What's up, Toyama?

637
41:01.363 --> 41:02.231
Huh?

638
41:06.802 --> 41:07.970
Where is she?

639
41:09.405 --> 41:12.041
-She's not here. -Mr. Tokushige!

640
41:12.107 --> 41:13.208
Natsuki!

641
41:13.776 --> 41:15.511
Over here!

642
41:15.578 --> 41:17.212
Toyama!

643
41:17.279 --> 41:19.515
Hey! What are you doing?

644
41:19.582 --> 41:23.419
We thought maybe we could run the cable along the river underneath.

645
41:23.485 --> 41:26.021
We've been trying all morning.

646
41:26.088 --> 41:27.623
We got it connected!

647
41:27.690 --> 41:28.958
Pull the cable!

648
41:29.024 --> 41:30.192
Okay!

649
41:35.931 --> 41:37.466
Don't get it wet!

650
41:37.533 --> 41:38.934
-Yes! -Yes!

651
41:43.906 --> 41:46.408
-Hey! Bring down the rope! -Okay!

652
41:49.678 --> 41:50.846
-Okay! -Yes!

653
41:57.720 --> 41:59.655
Okay, pull it up!

654
41:59.722 --> 42:00.623
Okay!

655
42:10.933 --> 42:13.802
All right! We did it!

656
42:14.470 --> 42:16.205
Huh? Okay?

657
42:16.805 --> 42:18.574
Really?

658
42:18.641 --> 42:21.477
Yes!

659
42:21.543 --> 42:23.379
We solved the cable problem!

660
42:23.445 --> 42:26.715
No, we still have to test it! We don't know yet!

661
42:26.782 --> 42:28.584
Never give up!

662
42:29.385 --> 42:30.953
Never give up!

663
42:31.020 --> 42:32.554
Miyamoto.

664
42:33.522 --> 42:36.592
You even won over that stubborn old man.

665
42:36.659 --> 42:39.194
Hey! Let's go!

666
42:45.200 --> 42:46.268
Here we go.

667
42:48.570 --> 42:49.972
Pull back a little more.

668
42:51.106 --> 42:52.541
Okay. That's good.

669
42:54.243 --> 42:58.280
Here we go. Ready, go!

670
43:00.949 --> 43:02.651
-Oh! -Oh!

671
43:02.718 --> 43:04.453
That's so easy to follow!

672
43:04.520 --> 43:07.956
You can see the distance between runners at a glance.

673
43:08.590 --> 43:09.625
If we can show

674
43:09.692 --> 43:13.062
a close race right before the sash relay with this shot…

675
43:14.163 --> 43:15.130
It'll be perfect.

676
43:17.499 --> 43:18.667
-Mr. Kuwana. -Huh?

677
43:19.234 --> 43:21.704
Thank you so much!

678
43:26.175 --> 43:27.076
Yeah.

679
43:27.142 --> 43:29.878
Mr. Tokushige, you're surprisingly fast.

680
43:31.180 --> 43:34.283
I'm Dainichi TV's Maradona.

681
43:34.349 --> 43:35.784
Maradona?

682
43:36.351 --> 43:37.419
Well…

683
43:38.053 --> 43:42.257
I might cry if I see this shot on air.

684
43:43.659 --> 43:44.560
Still,

685
43:45.294 --> 43:47.262
if we use it, it'll be two or three cuts.

686
43:47.329 --> 43:48.797
Two or three cuts?

687
43:49.398 --> 43:51.233
Talk about a waste!

688
43:52.201 --> 43:53.035
MEISEI GAKUIN UNIV.

689
43:53.102 --> 43:53.936
TRACK AND FIELD DORM

690
43:54.002 --> 43:55.471
Mr. Onuma, sorry.

691
43:55.537 --> 43:56.972
Do you have a minute?

692
43:58.373 --> 43:59.475
Yes.

693
44:01.276 --> 44:02.478
Mr. Kitano says

694
44:04.113 --> 44:06.181
he wants to resign as coach.

695
44:17.726 --> 44:22.064
I'm thinking of quitting as coach of the Student United Team.

696
44:23.432 --> 44:24.266
Huh?

697
44:25.968 --> 44:27.369
You quit too.

698
44:30.139 --> 44:32.074
You've got to be joking!

699
44:47.923 --> 44:49.324
If anything happens

700
44:50.025 --> 44:52.294
you said you'd take responsibility, right, Mr. Harada?

701
44:52.861 --> 44:54.763
I don't remember that.

702
45:02.070 --> 45:02.905
40 DAYS TO HAKONE EKIDEN

703
45:02.971 --> 45:04.006
THIS DRAMA IS FICTION. NO CONNECTION TO REAL PEOPLE OR GROUPS.
